# The image is built with `COPY . .` (Dockerfile), so whatever is in this directory goes into a
# layer — and a layer is copied to a registry, shared and cached. A developer who has filled in
# apps/api/.env to run `docker compose up` against a real Supabase project would otherwise bake the
# SERVICE-ROLE key and every AI key into the image. Compose passes them at RUN time through
# `env_file`, which is the only way they should arrive.
#
# `.env.*` also matches .env.example, which no running process reads.
#
# Render builds from a checkout, where the gitignored .env does not exist, so this changes nothing
# there. tests/test_docker_compose_names_only_what_a_clean_clone_has.py holds it.
.env
.env.*
__pycache__/
*.pyc
.pytest_cache/

# WHAT THE SERVING IMAGE DOES NOT NEED (engineering-04). The test suite is most of the directory's weight
# and none of it runs in production: shipping it put the test runner's whole fixture set, and every
# production-schema snapshot in tests/fixtures, into the image Render serves traffic from. Nothing the
# image runs imports from tests/ (a test asserts it, so a runtime module that starts to would fail CI
# instead of failing in production).
#
# migrations/ is NOT here and must not be: core/schema_guard.py reads the migration files at boot to
# know which columns the code expects. scripts/ is kept (small, and seed_demo_firm.py is run from a shell
# in the image). tests/test_the_backend_installs_from_a_lock.py holds both halves.
tests/
requirements.in
requirements-dev.in
requirements-dev.txt
